---
import AboutArticle from "../../components/AboutArticle.astro";
import { getAboutPage } from "../../lib/aboutPages";
import { getAboutHeroPhoto } from "../../lib/aboutPhotos";

const page = getAboutPage("esp32-ota-firmware")!;
const hero = getAboutHeroPhoto(page.slug);
---
<AboutArticle
  slug={page.slug}
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  heroImage={hero?.image}
  heroAlt={hero?.alt}
  heroCaption={hero?.caption}
  heroCredit={hero?.credit}
  heroCreditHref={hero?.sourceUrl}
>
  <p>
    ThermalTrace cares about <strong>readings over HTTPS</strong>, not how you update the MCU.
    Keep OTA on your LAN; keep ingest pointed at
    <code>/api/ingest/&lt;key&gt;</code>. Download a pre-filled sketch from
    <a class="text-link" href="/dashboard/devices">Devices</a> after you create a push device.
  </p>

  <h2>First flash vs OTA</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>USB first flash</strong>, use the Devices callout checklist (ESP32 board package, libraries,
      Serial 115200). See
      <a class="text-link" href="/about/esp32-web-flash">flashing options</a> for Arduino IDE, PlatformIO,
      MicroPython, and Espressif’s web esptool.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>LAN OTA later</strong>: once Wi‑Fi works, <code>ArduinoOTA</code> (or ESP-IDF HTTPS OTA) lets you
      push new firmware without climbing back to the probe with a cable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>ArduinoOTA (LAN)</h2>
  <p>
    After <code>WiFi.begin</code> succeeds, start OTA and call <code>handle()</code> in <code>loop</code>.
    Use a password in production shops. PlatformIO / Arduino IDE “network port” then shows the board.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`#include <ArduinoOTA.h>

void setup() {
  // … WiFi connected …
  ArduinoOTA.setHostname("garage-probe");
  ArduinoOTA.setPassword("change-me");
  ArduinoOTA.begin();
}

void loop() {
  ArduinoOTA.handle();
  // … your ingest POST on an interval …
}`}</code></pre>
  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
    OTA and ingest are independent: a failed cloud POST does not block OTA, and an OTA update does not
    change your ThermalTrace device key.
  </p>

  <h2>Ingest payload with battery and RSSI</h2>
  <p>
    Optional top-level fields keep Devices health and low-battery / weak-signal alerts accurate:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "temp1": 42.5,
  "door1": false,
  "battery": 87,
  "rssi": -62
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The DS18B20 samples already send <code>rssi</code>. Add <code>battery</code> or
    <code>battery_pct</code> when you have a voltage divider or fuel gauge. Enable health alerts under
    <a class="text-link" href="/dashboard/alerts">Dashboard → Alerts</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Wi‑Fi without recompiling</h2>
  <p>
    Hardcoded <code>WIFI_SSID</code> / <code>WIFI_PASS</code> is fine for a fixed garage AP. If the network
    changes often, use the
    <a
      class="text-link"
      href="https://github.com/doodersrage/thermaltrace/tree/main/sketches/arduino/ds18b20_wifimanager"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noreferrer"
    >WiFiManager sketch</a>
    captive portal on first boot; ingest URL still comes from the Devices download or a
    <code>#define</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/about/adding-devices">Adding devices</a></li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/about/esp32-web-flash">ESP32 flashing options</a></li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/about/kit-qr-onboarding">Kit QR onboarding</a></li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/docs/api#ingest">HTTP API: ingest</a></li>
  </ul>
</AboutArticle>
